gyD(){return"Your Road to 100 Push Ups"},
gyl(){return"We'll build a personalized daily plan that takes you from where you are today to 100 consecutive push ups \u2014 no gym, no equipment, just a few minutes a day."},
gyr(){return"Chest, shoulders, triceps, and core \u2014 a classic compound push movement that builds upper-body pressing strength and full-body stability."},
gyf(){return"No equipment, no gym membership, works anywhere \u2014 one of the most efficient ways to build real upper-body strength using just your bodyweight."},
gyx(){return"100 consecutive push ups is a widely recognized benchmark of upper-body endurance \u2014 most people who train consistently for a few months can reach it, regardless of where they start."},
gyG(){return"Your Road to 100 Squats"},
gyo(){return"We'll build a personalized daily plan that takes you from your current max to 100 consecutive squats \u2014 just your bodyweight and a few minutes a day."},
gyu(){return"Quads, glutes, and hamstrings \u2014 the squat is the foundation movement for lower-body strength and everyday functional mobility."},
gyi(){return"Builds lower-body strength and mobility that carries over to walking, climbing stairs, and everyday movement \u2014 no equipment required."},
gyA(){return"100 consecutive squats is a well-known strength-endurance milestone \u2014 achievable by most people with a gradual, progressive plan, even starting from zero."},
gyC(){return"Your Road to 50 Pull Ups"},
gyk(){return"We'll build a personalized daily plan that takes you from your current max to 50 consecutive pull ups \u2014 the plan adapts to your own pace."},
gyq(){return"Back, biceps, and grip \u2014 one of the toughest bodyweight pulling movements, and one of the best builders of true upper-body pulling strength."},
gye(){return"Pull ups train a muscle group most pushing-focused routines neglect \u2014 a stronger back and grip translate directly into better posture and pulling power in daily life."},
gyw(){return"50 consecutive pull ups is an elite-level benchmark \u2014 few people ever reach it, so your plan sets realistic intermediate milestones along the way instead of expecting a straight line from day one."},
gyB(){return"Your Road to a 5-Minute Plank"},
gyj(){return"We'll build a personalized daily plan that takes you from your current hold time to a full 5-minute plank \u2014 a few focused minutes a day, growing gradually."},
gyp(){return"Core, shoulders, and glutes \u2014 an isometric hold that builds deep core stability and postural endurance, not just visible abs."},
gyd(){return"Core stability is the foundation almost every other movement relies on \u2014 a stronger plank means a stronger, more protected lower back in everything else you do."},
gyv(){return"Holding a plank for 5 straight minutes is a recognized core-endurance benchmark, reached through steady week-over-week increases rather than any single big jump."},
gyE(){return"Your Road to a 10K Run"},
gym(){return"We'll build a personalized daily plan that takes you from your current distance to running 10 kilometers straight \u2014 gradual mileage increases, with rest days built in."},
gys(){return"Cardiovascular endurance and leg strength \u2014 running trains your whole aerobic system, not just one muscle group."},
gyg(){return"Running is one of the most accessible ways to build cardiovascular health \u2014 no equipment beyond a pair of shoes, and it's directly tied to long-term heart and metabolic health."},
gyy(){return"10 kilometers is the classic distance milestone for a recreational runner \u2014 a realistic, well-established target for someone building up from a shorter starting distance."},
gyF(){return"Your Road to 200 Sit Ups"},
gyn(){return"We'll build a personalized daily plan that takes you from your current max to 200 consecutive sit ups \u2014 just your bodyweight and a few minutes a day."},
gyt(){return"Abdominals and hip flexors \u2014 a classic core-endurance movement that builds trunk strength through a full range of motion."},
gyh(){return"Builds core endurance that supports posture and protects your lower back during almost every other physical activity \u2014 no equipment required."},
gyz(){return"200 consecutive sit ups is a demanding but achievable core-endurance milestone \u2014 the high rep count is exactly why the plan builds toward it gradually instead of all at once."},
gyJ(){return"Uses your phone's proximity sensor. Place it face-up on the floor, right where your face or chest lowers to during the push up. Getting close enough and pulling back again counts as one rep."},
gyM(){return"Uses your phone's accelerometer. Keep it in your front pants or shorts pocket while you squat. Going down and back up counts as one rep."},
gyL(){return"Uses your phone's accelerometer. Hold it with both hands against your chest while you sit up. Curling up and back down counts as one rep."},
gyI(){return"Uses your phone's accelerometer (experimental). Keep it in a snug pocket or a sports armband. The range of motion is smaller than the other exercises, so it can miss more often \u2014 check the count and adjust it with +/- if needed."},
gyH(){return"Uses your phone's accelerometer. Place it screen-up on your lower back, or in a snug back pocket. The timer only runs while your torso is horizontal: if you stand up or drop to your knees, it stops until you're back in position. It detects the position, not your form \u2014 lying flat on the floor looks the same."},
gyK(){return"Uses your phone's GPS. Carry it with you (pocket, armband or hand) and run outdoors: distance is measured automatically and the run ends when you reach the goal. It keeps measuring with the screen off; you'll see a notification meanwhile. Indoors or between tall buildings the signal is weaker and may undercount."},
